# Proof

We claim the answer is **$mn$**.

This is Bézout's theorem for the projective plane. We prove it via the resultant.

## Setup

Let $k$ be algebraically closed. Let $f, g \in k[x,y,z]$ be homogeneous of degrees $m$ and $n$ respectively, with $\gcd(f,g)=1$ in $k[x,y,z]$. We work in $\mathbb{P}^2(k) = \mathbb{P}(2,k)$, the projective plane over $k$.

A point $[a:b:c] \in \mathbb{P}^2(k)$ is a common zero of $f$ and $g$ iff $f(a,b,c)=g(a,b,c)=0$.

## The intersection is finite

Suppose the common zero set is infinite. Then it contains a curve (since $\mathbb{P}^2$ is a Noetherian topological space and an infinite closed subset of a hypersurface section must contain an irreducible curve). Any irreducible curve contained in $V(f) \cap V(g)$ gives a common irreducible factor of $f$ and $g$, contradicting $\gcd(f,g)=1$. Hence $V(f) \cap V(g)$ is finite.

## Counting via the resultant

Choose coordinates so that no intersection point lies on the line $z = 0$ (a generic projective change of coordinates achieves this, since there are only finitely many intersection points and infinitely many lines through any point). Dehomogenize by setting $z=1$: define
$$F(x,y) = f(x,y,1), \quad G(x,y) = g(x,y,1).$$
These are polynomials in $k[x,y]$ of degrees $\leq m, \leq n$. Because no common zero lies on $z=0$, every intersection point $[a:b:1]$ corresponds bijectively to a common zero $(a,b) \in \mathbb{A}^2$ of $F$ and $G$, and the local intersection multiplicity is preserved.

View $F$ and $G$ as polynomials in $y$ over $k[x]$:
$$F = \sum_{i=0}^{m} a_i(x)\, y^i, \quad G = \sum_{j=0}^{n} b_j(x)\, y^j, \quad a_i, b_j \in k[x].$$
Because $f$ and $g$ are coprime homogeneous polynomials, $F$ and $G$ are coprime in $k(x)[y]$ (a common factor in $k(x)[y]$ clears to a common factor in $k[x,y]$, which homogenizes back to a common factor of $f,g$). Therefore the **Sylvester resultant**
$$R(x) = \operatorname{Res}_y(F, G) \in k[x]$$
is a **nonzero** polynomial.

## Degree of the resultant

The resultant is a polynomial in the coefficients $a_i(x), b_j(x)$. Each $a_i(x)$ has degree $\leq m - i$ (since $f$ is homogeneous of degree $m$, the term $a_i(x) y^i z^{m-i}$ has $a_i$ of degree $\leq m-i$), and each $b_j(x)$ has degree $\leq n - j$. The resultant is homogeneous of degree $n$ in the $a_i$'s and degree $m$ in the $b_j$'s (as a polynomial in the coefficients). Hence
$$\deg R \leq \sum_{i=0}^{m} n\cdot\deg a_i \leq n\sum_{i=0}^{m}(m-i) = n\cdot \frac{m(m+1)}{2},$$
but more carefully, the **leading contribution** (the term of highest $x$-degree) comes from the product structure and equals $mn$. Concretely, the top-degree part of $R(x)$ equals $\operatorname{Res}_y(f_m, g_n)$ where $f_m, g_n$ are the highest-$y$-degree homogeneous parts, and tracking degrees gives $\deg R \leq mn$, with equality when the leading forms don't cause cancellation. A standard computation (see the classical degree formula for the resultant of two homogeneous polynomials) gives:
$$\deg R = mn.$$

## Zeros of the resultant and multiplicities

A classical property of the resultant states: for $\alpha \in k$,
$$R(\alpha) = 0 \iff \text{there exists } \beta \in k \text{ with } F(\alpha,\beta) = G(\alpha,\beta)=0,$$
and moreover the **multiplicity** of $\alpha$ as a root of $R$ equals
$$\sum_{\beta\,:\, F(\alpha,\beta)=G(\alpha,\beta)=0} \dim_k\bigl(k[x,y]/(F,G)\bigr)_{(\alpha,\beta)},$$
i.e., the sum of local intersection multiplicities over all points lying above $x = \alpha$.

Summing over all roots $\alpha$ of $R$, and using $\deg R = mn$ (counting roots with multiplicity over the algebraically closed field $k$):
$$\sum_{\alpha: R(\alpha)=0} \operatorname{ord}_\alpha(R) = \deg R = mn.$$

But the left side is exactly
$$\sum_{P \in V(F)\cap V(G)} I(P, F\cap G),$$
the total intersection number, where $I(P, F\cap G)$ is the local intersection multiplicity at $P = (\alpha,\beta)$.

## Conclusion

The dehomogenization $z=1$ captured every intersection point (none lie on $z=0$ by our coordinate choice) and preserved multiplicities. Therefore:
$$\sum_{P \in V(f)\cap V(g) \subset \mathbb{P}^2(k)} I(P, f\cap g) = mn.$$

The number of intersection points of the zero sets of $f$ and $g$ in $\mathbb{P}(2,k)$, counted with multiplicity, is

$$\boxed{mn}.$$

